For what is worth with the same version of sysklogd package I see
the following after HUP to syslogd:

select(1, [0], NULL, NULL, NULL)        = ? ERESTARTNOHAND (To be restarted)
--- SIGHUP (Hangup) ---
sigaction(SIGHUP, {0x120006788, [], SA_RESTART}, {0x120006788, [], SA_RESTART}, 0x1200067b4) = 0
sigreturn()                             = ? (mask now [])
SYS_5(0x200002fce58, 0, 0x1b6, 0, 0x400) = 92
SYS_0(0x5, 0x1, 0, 0, 0x400)            = 92
SYS_0(0x5, 0x2, 0x1, 0, 0x400)          = -1 EL3RST (Level 3 reset)
SYS_0(0x5, 0x11fffd490, 0x11fffd510, 0, 0) = -1 EREMOTE (Object is remote)
read(5, "#\n# services\tThis file describes"..., 8192) = 4551
...................
fsync(2)                                = 0
sigaction(SIGHUP, {0x120006788, [], SA_RESTART}, {0x120006788, [], SA_RESTART}, 0x120005740) = 0
select(1, [0], NULL, NULL, NULL)        = ? ERESTARTNOHAND (To be restarted)
--- SIGALRM (Alarm clock) ---
sigaction(SIGALRM, {0x120004a78, [], SA_RESTART}, {0x120004a78, [], SA_RESTART}, 0x120004bf0) = 0
setitimer(ITIMER_REAL, {it_interval={0, 0}, it_value={30, 0}}, {it_interval={0, 0}, it_value={0, 0}}) = 0
sigreturn()                             = ? (mask now [])


and syslogd happilly runs after re-reading its config and restarting logs.
Maybe this will help to find out where is your problem?

  Michal

p.s.  Why are you qouting messages in this annoyingly unreadable
"Jeopardy style"?  Too much TV?




